<h2 id="Introduction">Introduction<a class="anchor-link" href="#Introduction">¶</a></h2><p>In Module 6, we built and evaluated a Linear Regression model to predict the median house values across California using housing and demographic data from the U.S. Census Bureau. The model leveraged key features like median income, location coordinates, and household attributes to estimate property values, achieving moderate predictive performance.</p>
<p>However, while numerical metrics such as R², MSE, and RMSE help assess the accuracy of the model, they often fall short in explaining how different housing segments relate to one another in a multidimensional space. This is where data visualization becomes a powerful tool. It allows us to not only interpret model outcomes but also uncover hidden patterns, clusters, and non-linear relationships that the model might have leveraged—or missed.</p>
<p>In this module, our goal is to take the predictive output and transform it into visually interpretable segments. To do so, we start by dividing housing prices into three meaningful categories—Low, Medium, and High—based on logical pricing thresholds. We then apply three popular dimensionality reduction techniques—Principal Component Analysis (PCA), Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection (UMAP), and t-distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding (t-SNE)—to visualize how well these categories cluster based on the top 10 most influential features from our regression model.</p>
<p>By reducing the complexity of high-dimensional data into two dimensions, we aim to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Reveal the internal structure of the housing market,</li>
<li>Better understand the interactions between influential features,</li>
<li>And visually validate how separable different price ranges are.</li>
</ul>
<p>These insights are particularly useful for policymakers, real estate investors, data scientists, and urban planners aiming to understand spatial and socioeconomic factors that shape the housing landscape of California.</p>
