<h1 id="Conclusion">Conclusion<a class="anchor-link" href="#Conclusion">¶</a></h1><p>In this module, we extended our California housing price prediction project by focusing on visualization and interpretation of the model’s outcomes. By transforming the continuous target variable (median_house_value) into three meaningful price categories—Low, Medium, and High—we enabled a clearer understanding of price segmentation across different housing regions.</p>
<p>We applied three powerful dimensionality reduction techniques—PCA, UMAP, and t-SNE—using the top 10 features identified in our linear regression model. Each technique helped reduce the complexity of our dataset to two dimensions for visual analysis:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>PCA offered a quick, linear projection showing moderate separation between price categories.</p>
</li>
<li><p>UMAP effectively captured both local and global structure, revealing clearer boundaries between Low, Medium, and High price segments.</p>
</li>
<li><p>t-SNE produced the most distinct clusters, highlighting non-linear relationships and natural groupings in the data.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>These visualizations allowed us to validate the predictive importance of features like median income, location, and proximity to the ocean, while also providing a powerful tool to communicate insights to non-technical stakeholders.</p>
<p>Overall, Module 7 demonstrated the value of visualization in understanding machine learning outcomes, uncovering hidden patterns, and enhancing the interpretability of complex, multidimensional housing data.</p>
